Exploring FFAT Storage
Spear Field · Hostess 7 Field Press
====================================

Field Fat (FFAT) is entropy-packed field storage. It is not Microsoft FAT16/32.
This book is the field operator's guide: what FFAT is, what it refuses to pretend,
and how to measure capacity honestly.

Chapter 1 — What FFAT Is
------------------------
FFAT (Field Fat) is a field storage layout used by Spear / Hostess 7 for field drives
and related media. On-disk identity uses field magics:

  SPEARMBR     — master boot record tag (field tech)
  FFAT\x03ENT  — superblock magic (entropy edition v4)
  FDRV\x01     — Field Drive identity seal

Legacy superblocks may appear as FFAT\x02FLD. Those are recognized as present but
obsolete. The field stack never treats "MSDOS5.0" / host vfat as FFAT truth.

Frames are the unit of work. Default field frame size is 4096 bytes. Physical media
is still addressed in 512-byte sectors; clusters and partitions follow field geometry
(e.g. partition type 0xE0 for Field Fat, first partition LBA often 2048 for 1 MiB align).

Chapter 2 — Honesty First (Shannon Capacity)
--------------------------------------------
Field storage follows Primer honesty (Shannon H and lossless bounds):

  H = −Σ pᵢ log₂ pᵢ    bits per byte on a frame histogram (range 0..8)

A lossless store of a frame must spend at least (H/8) · frame_bytes physical bytes
for that frame's information content. Capacity gain is MEASURED from what the packer
actually achieves on real data:

  ZERO  — all-zero logical frames (no pool bytes)
  RLE   — run-length packed frames
  PAK   — peak–angle pack (ramps / peaks / valleys)
  REF   — content-dedup reference to another logical frame
  DICT  — shared pattern dictionary (host + GPU kin)
  RAW   — full frame when packing cannot win

NEVER invent a hardcoded multiplier. The old ×91 "fake metadata" superblock is
rejected and rewritten away. If you see legacy_fake_factor, the volume was lying;
field tools repair it to honest accounting.

Chapter 3 — Accounting Words That Mean Something
------------------------------------------------
Operators must use the same words the volume uses:

  physical_bytes       media size
  guaranteed_bytes     honest worst-case usable pool (all unique high-H RAW frames)
  address_space_bytes  sparse logical map · frame size (empty costs map only)
  logical_stored       sum of logical payload represented
  physical_used        pool bytes actually written
  pack_ratio           logical_stored / physical_used  (live, after writes)
  shannon_avg          mean H over measured frames (bits/byte)

pack_ratio_x1000 is stored as integer thousandths (1000 = 1.0×). It is a measurement
after writes, not a marketing claim printed at format time.

Chapter 4 — Peak–Angle Pack (PAK)
---------------------------------
PAK models a field signal path: more logical samples than stored control points.
Between peaks, angle-up and angle-down (constant-step ramps) reconstruct the path.
One PEAK or VALLEY record can cover many samples. Prefer PEAK/VALLEY (most samples
per control point), then RAMP, FLAT, LIT when choosing encodings.

PAK is for structured field signals — not a license to claim infinite density on
noise. High Shannon H frames fall back toward RAW. That is correct behavior.

Chapter 5 — Map Entry Kinds
---------------------------
Logical map entries are typed:

  FREE 0   unused logical slot
  ZERO 1   all-zero frame (0 pool bytes)
  RAW  2   full frame in pool
  RLE  3   RLE-packed in pool
  REF  4   content-dedup → other logical id
  PAK  5   peak–angle pack
  DICT 6   shared pattern dictionary

Reading a logical frame unpacks by kind. Writing chooses the best honest pack for
that frame and records kind + Shannon measurement.

Chapter 6 — Virtual AMMOFAT Geometry (Bus Only)
-----------------------------------------------
Primer ch.08 fatPack describes virtual AMMOFAT geometry on the field bus — not a
host vfat filesystem. Typical defaults:

  bytes per sector     512
  sectors per cluster  8   (field frame / 512)
  reserved sectors     1
  FATs                 2
  root entries         512

Host tools must not mount FFAT volumes as MS-DOS FAT. Field Die fatPack geometry
lives on bus indices; Field Drive identity uses the FDRV seal.

Chapter 7 — Field Drive Identity
--------------------------------
A Field Drive is more than free space. Identity tags bind the media to field law:

  FDRV family seal
  volume label (field string)
  secure tag SECURE01 when required

ffat_probe and ffat_ensure are the C++ entry points (Spear):

  ffat_probe(path)           inspect without writing
  ffat_ensure(path, force)   create/repair honest superblock + map
  ffat_put_frame / get_frame logical frame IO on a formatted volume

map_divisor defaults to 32 for disks; field memory planes may use 8 for denser
sparse address space (more field-logical memory per physical pool byte).

Chapter 8 — Operator Rituals
----------------------------
1. Probe before format. Know whether you have FFAT v4, legacy, or foreign media.
2. Reject ×91 and other fake capacity. Prefer guaranteed_bytes over fairy tales.
3. After writes, read pack_ratio and shannon_avg. Those are the truth of the day.
4. Seal shots and doctrine separately — storage honesty is not wartime theater.
5. Scripts are not the product path for Spear field tools. C++ / lower owns runtime.

Chapter 9 — Relationship to Hostess 7 Library
---------------------------------------------
This book sits on Dewey shelf 005 (computer programs & data) alongside field
guides and format registry cards. Hostess 7 holds the books and shares them.
The text may be stored as Hostess 7 Condenser (H7c) for lossless field packing —
combinatronic + zlib — without changing the meaning of a single character.

Chapter 10 — Motto
------------------
Measure entropy. Pack only when pack wins. Never invent capacity.
Exploring FFAT Storage is complete when the operator can explain every byte of
guaranteed_bytes without a story.

God Bless.
